[{:db/id 17592186092373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a42296f-57b5-52e8-abcc-dbabb67e9573" "question-2855547f-5d51-5448-b3d2-5e4f3624a47a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-53d69396-ad38-5399-a95d-de36a99debd9" "question-496b1e54-6c94-5491-8b44-b97586fa418a" "question-f471af72-8928-5fe0-b630-625d8128766a" "question-954fde1b-bd90-5ee0-826c-b4121bc79ca3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a42296f-57b5-52e8-abcc-dbabb67e9573" :question/id #uuid "7a42296f-57b5-52e8-abcc-dbabb67e9573" :question/math-academy-id "q-15121" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}+(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48332077-b3d7-5599-9447-7b3caa313948"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48332077-b3d7-5599-9447-7b3caa313948" :answer-field/id #uuid "48332077-b3d7-5599-9447-7b3caa313948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-71985b22-a29a-5e8e-a361-5aa92a2495cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0934e304-801c-5c0d-8b01-386d6337214b" "answer-b8da428a-8a49-5ad1-b789-ffb6514c0b70" "answer-33a0bdd4-e800-5d6d-849a-fb395e74e06a" "answer-71985b22-a29a-5e8e-a361-5aa92a2495cd" "answer-cdce5e1a-03fe-5258-9d66-a128329565d7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0934e304-801c-5c0d-8b01-386d6337214b" :answer/id #uuid "0934e304-801c-5c0d-8b01-386d6337214b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta ))^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8da428a-8a49-5ad1-b789-ffb6514c0b70" :answer/id #uuid "b8da428a-8a49-5ad1-b789-ffb6514c0b70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5\\theta )+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33a0bdd4-e800-5d6d-849a-fb395e74e06a" :answer/id #uuid "33a0bdd4-e800-5d6d-849a-fb395e74e06a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}({sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71985b22-a29a-5e8e-a361-5aa92a2495cd" :answer/id #uuid "71985b22-a29a-5e8e-a361-5aa92a2495cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+25{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdce5e1a-03fe-5258-9d66-a128329565d7" :answer/id #uuid "cdce5e1a-03fe-5258-9d66-a128329565d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /5}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )+5{cos}^{2}⁡(5\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2855547f-5d51-5448-b3d2-5e4f3624a47a" :question/id #uuid "2855547f-5d51-5448-b3d2-5e4f3624a47a" :question/math-academy-id "q-51199" :question/problem "![](images/50/5052773d05690f49969487f5ec75a1cdd15c278c1c7552dd5d811ea2e023256c.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}+(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+1+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a3650c47-b992-5daf-9894-5a44218a3232"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a3650c47-b992-5daf-9894-5a44218a3232" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3650c47-b992-5daf-9894-5a44218a3232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7aa86582-4f58-56be-8b2e-64523909f390" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b917d8d-ed20-53e9-8f2f-10a316604da1" "answer-bc99ca06-7d50-5d19-b832-2a97e3ac7a8e" "answer-7aa86582-4f58-56be-8b2e-64523909f390" "answer-d281e271-ac5c-5125-9398-f8102c7c4a4a" "answer-10cd98c4-ea24-55c4-a79c-cf3c9ddf5714"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b917d8d-ed20-53e9-8f2f-10a316604da1" :answer/id #uuid "7b917d8d-ed20-53e9-8f2f-10a316604da1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc99ca06-7d50-5d19-b832-2a97e3ac7a8e" :answer/id #uuid "bc99ca06-7d50-5d19-b832-2a97e3ac7a8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aa86582-4f58-56be-8b2e-64523909f390" :answer/id #uuid "7aa86582-4f58-56be-8b2e-64523909f390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d281e271-ac5c-5125-9398-f8102c7c4a4a" :answer/id #uuid "d281e271-ac5c-5125-9398-f8102c7c4a4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10cd98c4-ea24-55c4-a79c-cf3c9ddf5714" :answer/id #uuid "10cd98c4-ea24-55c4-a79c-cf3c9ddf5714" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-53d69396-ad38-5399-a95d-de36a99debd9" :question/id #uuid "53d69396-ad38-5399-a95d-de36a99debd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103848" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r={4}^{\\theta }$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={4}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }={4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({4}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{({4}^{\\theta }\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{4}^{2\\theta }+{4}^{2\\theta }{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1+{ln}^{2}⁡4){4}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{4}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot {4}^{\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\cdot ({4}^{\\pi /2}-{4}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e112384-cbab-5d5f-b556-741daf47c239"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e112384-cbab-5d5f-b556-741daf47c239" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e112384-cbab-5d5f-b556-741daf47c239" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41e5d50b-2d0d-5687-83c8-c9e67c35fd0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41e5d50b-2d0d-5687-83c8-c9e67c35fd0e" "answer-fa0493e8-3b12-50f8-9fc2-390aa24a534b" "answer-13998c82-80d9-5cc7-a5a5-a3c689f79c8d" "answer-05141a1b-cb9e-5283-9e45-82ca469a01e7" "answer-bb736353-f883-5d04-8785-134a0e8d1393"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41e5d50b-2d0d-5687-83c8-c9e67c35fd0e" :answer/id #uuid "41e5d50b-2d0d-5687-83c8-c9e67c35fd0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa0493e8-3b12-50f8-9fc2-390aa24a534b" :answer/id #uuid "fa0493e8-3b12-50f8-9fc2-390aa24a534b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13998c82-80d9-5cc7-a5a5-a3c689f79c8d" :answer/id #uuid "13998c82-80d9-5cc7-a5a5-a3c689f79c8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05141a1b-cb9e-5283-9e45-82ca469a01e7" :answer/id #uuid "05141a1b-cb9e-5283-9e45-82ca469a01e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{1+{ln}^{2}⁡4}({4}^{\\pi /2}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb736353-f883-5d04-8785-134a0e8d1393" :answer/id #uuid "bb736353-f883-5d04-8785-134a0e8d1393" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({4}^{\\pi /2}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-496b1e54-6c94-5491-8b44-b97586fa418a" :question/id #uuid "496b1e54-6c94-5491-8b44-b97586fa418a" :question/math-academy-id "q-353382" :question/problem "![](images/07/07dd1e37f29e06051c7fe84210d41857c7c871ce577c71a86781be4ce7a0ef23.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(7\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +49{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{49}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}7\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =7(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e0e99ea-5dd8-556e-b3e4-bb6d0549ec0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e0e99ea-5dd8-556e-b3e4-bb6d0549ec0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e0e99ea-5dd8-556e-b3e4-bb6d0549ec0e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7b4071d8-b7bd-5947-891a-3136a4368de9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b4071d8-b7bd-5947-891a-3136a4368de9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b4071d8-b7bd-5947-891a-3136a4368de9" :answer/id #uuid "7b4071d8-b7bd-5947-891a-3136a4368de9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f471af72-8928-5fe0-b630-625d8128766a" :question/id #uuid "f471af72-8928-5fe0-b630-625d8128766a" :question/math-academy-id "q-347923" :question/problem "![](images/81/81632c931b0846520a056a872cdf327b9031fea4e3b91bf2374973fff1a2354b.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the polar curve $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above). Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{(3+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3i}\\sqrt{18+18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =3\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={6\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =12(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0b76675c-e686-504c-9fa2-4f4538262ff5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0b76675c-e686-504c-9fa2-4f4538262ff5" :answer-field/id #uuid "0b76675c-e686-504c-9fa2-4f4538262ff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3ebaec30-3483-55e2-9524-7055da0607e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3ebaec30-3483-55e2-9524-7055da0607e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ebaec30-3483-55e2-9524-7055da0607e1" :answer/id #uuid "3ebaec30-3483-55e2-9524-7055da0607e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-954fde1b-bd90-5ee0-826c-b4121bc79ca3" :question/id #uuid "954fde1b-bd90-5ee0-826c-b4121bc79ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-9538" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={2(-2)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =4-2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f44e970c-d097-5c6c-a97c-5ef5ce4b1558"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f44e970c-d097-5c6c-a97c-5ef5ce4b1558" :answer-field/id #uuid "f44e970c-d097-5c6c-a97c-5ef5ce4b1558" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fb78dc11-cc0d-583f-b9e3-429b039a2c4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5d38d23-26ec-5c2a-a257-830016a32749" "answer-dde56d65-c679-5147-bb10-2f5b7354e62d" "answer-91b14c4c-3ed0-52f0-a3ee-ae78598ce9a2" "answer-8ea5acb7-0ee2-5efa-9cb6-56917fa73a24" "answer-fb78dc11-cc0d-583f-b9e3-429b039a2c4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5d38d23-26ec-5c2a-a257-830016a32749" :answer/id #uuid "f5d38d23-26ec-5c2a-a257-830016a32749" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dde56d65-c679-5147-bb10-2f5b7354e62d" :answer/id #uuid "dde56d65-c679-5147-bb10-2f5b7354e62d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91b14c4c-3ed0-52f0-a3ee-ae78598ce9a2" :answer/id #uuid "91b14c4c-3ed0-52f0-a3ee-ae78598ce9a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea5acb7-0ee2-5efa-9cb6-56917fa73a24" :answer/id #uuid "8ea5acb7-0ee2-5efa-9cb6-56917fa73a24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4+2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb78dc11-cc0d-583f-b9e3-429b039a2c4c" :answer/id #uuid "fb78dc11-cc0d-583f-b9e3-429b039a2c4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186093364 :tutorial/content "We've been using the following formula to compute the arc length bounded by a polar curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ r^2 + \\left( \\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} \\right)^2} \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? Let's start with the familiar formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nA polar curve is really just a parametric curve with parameter $\\theta$ and parametric equations given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the arc length formula for a parametric function with parameter $\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{ \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\textrm{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $x = r \\cos \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since $y=r\\sin \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $L,$ we reach the desired formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092470 :activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
